== cosets and quotients

Lagrange's partition from chapter 16 says the cosets of a subgroup $H$ tile $G$ into equal blocks. A quotient asks when the blocks themselves form a group under $(a H) dot (b H) = (a b) H$: exactly when left and right cosets agree, $g H = H g$ for every $g$, the normal subgroups (Wikipedia, "quotient group", fetched 2026-09-21: "A subgroup N of a group G is normal if and only if the coset equality aN = Na holds for all a in G").

The dry run: two subgroups of the abelian $Z_6$, where normality is free, one subgroup of $S_3$ where it fails.

+ $H = {0, 2, 4}$ tiles $Z_6$ into $H$ and $1 + H = {1, 3, 5}$. Adding blocks: $(1+H) + (1+H) = 2 + H = H$, a two-element group, isomorphic to $Z_2$. Every one of the 9 representative pairs lands in the same block, so the block sum does not depend on representatives.
+ $H = {0, 3}$ tiles $Z_6$ into ${0, 3}, {1, 4}, {2, 5}$, and three copies of $1 + H$ return to $H$: the quotient adds like $Z_3$.
+ In $S_3$ with $H = {e, "s01"}$: the left coset $r H = {r, "s02"}$ while the right coset $H r = {r, "s12"}$. Different sets, so $H$ is not normal.

The failure has a product witness, worth seeing once in full. Multiply the cosets $r H$ and $r^2 H$ elementwise: the four products $r dot r^2$, $r dot "s12"$, $"s02" dot r^2$, $"s02" dot "s12"$ are $e, "s01", "s12", r^2$, which sit in two different cosets at once, ${e, "s01"}$ and ${r^2, "s12"}$. The result is not a coset, so the candidate operation has no value: coset arithmetic on a non-normal subgroup is not merely inconvenient, it is undefined.

The repair inside $S_3$: the rotations $A_3 = {e, r, r^2}$ do satisfy $g A_3 = A_3 g$ for all six $g$, checks 11 and 12 of quotients.c, and the quotient works. The even permutations form one coset, the three reflections the other, and every odd times odd product is even on all 9 pairs: $S_3 \/ A_3$ is a group of order 2, isomorphic to $Z_2$, the sign homomorphism's kernel doing what section 1 promised.

