== rings and fields

A ring adds a second operation to a group. Definition: $(R, +, dot)$ is a ring when $R$ is an abelian group under $+$, multiplication is associative with an identity (for rings with 1), and distributivity $a dot (b + c) = a dot b + a dot c$ ties them together. A field is a commutative ring where every nonzero element has a multiplicative inverse.

The integers mod $n$ are the fixture ring, and the two operations disagree about $n$ in exactly one way. Under addition $Z_n$ is always a group. Under multiplication it is a field precisely when $n$ is prime, and the reason is the zero divisors: in $Z_6$, $2 dot 3 = 6 equiv 0$, and a zero divisor can never invert, because $x dot 2 equiv 1$ would give $3 dot x dot 2 equiv 3 dot 1 equiv 3$ on one side and $3 dot 0 equiv 0$ on the other.

The dry run: $Z_5$ inverts everything, $1 dot 1 = 1$, $2 dot 3 = 6 equiv 1$, $4 dot 4 = 16 equiv 1$. $Z_6$ keeps only 1 and 5, and the $Z_12$ census separates the rest: zero divisors $2, 3, 4, 6, 8, 9, 10$ against units $1, 5, 7, 11$, with $5 dot 5 = 25 equiv 1$, check 4 of fields.c. The scan over $n = 2$ to 12 pins the equivalence both directions, primes 2, 3, 5, 7, 11 have all nonzero residues invertible, every composite has a zero divisor pair, and distributivity holds on all 216 triples of $Z_6$, the hand triple $2 dot (3 + 5) = 16 equiv 4$ against $6 + 10 equiv 4$ among them.

