== the dihedral group d4 acts on a square

The symmetries of the square form $D_4$: four rotations, four reflections, each a permutation of the four vertices. With $r$ the quarter turn and $s$ the reflection fixing vertices 0 and 2, every element is one of $e, r, r^2, r^3, s, r s, r^2 s, r^3 s$, and two relations pin the whole group: $r^4 = s^2 = e$ and $s ∘ r = r^3 ∘ s$, pass an $s$ left across an $r$ and the $r$ inverts. That exchange rule is the non-commutativity in one line, and it is why the 64 products close without storing a table.

A group action is a group behaving like a machine on a set: each $g in G$ moves points $x -> g dot x$, with $e dot x = x$ and $g dot (h dot x) = (g ∘ h) dot x$. Two sets fall out. The orbit of $x$ is everything reachable, ${g dot x : g in G}$. The stabilizer of $x$ is everything that fixes it, ${g : g dot x = x}$, itself a subgroup.

The dry run: under the eight symmetries the orbit of vertex 0 is all 4 vertices, its stabilizer is exactly ${e, s}$, and $4 dot 2 = 8 = |D_4|$. That product is no coincidence, it is the orbit-stabilizer theorem, Lagrange counting twice: the cosets of the stabilizer biject with the orbit.

